    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d465">

                <pb facs="0494" n="322" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 862.20235/32a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-09-07T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 7, 1917, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Following statement by me which is furnished to you for your information and use
                    in event of official inquiry, but not to be given to press by you, will be
                    issued to press here at 2 p.m., September 8: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>The Department has secured certain telegrams from Count Luxburg, German
                            Chargé dAffaires at Buenos Aires, to the Foreign Office at Berlin,
                            which, I regret to say, were dispatched from Buenos Aires by the Swedish
                            Legation as their own official messages, addressed to the Stockholm
                            Foreign Office.</p>
                        <p>The following are English translations of the German text: <quote
                                rend="blockquote">
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#italic">May 19, 1917.</hi>
                                </p>
                                <p>No. 32. This Government has now released German and Austrian
                                    ships in which hitherto a guard had been placed. In consequence
                                    of the settlement of the <hi rendition="#italic">Monte</hi> (<hi
                                        rendition="#italic">Protegido</hi>) case, there has been a
                                    great change in public feeling. Government will in future only
                                    clear Argentine ships as far as Las Palmas. I beg that the small
                                    steamers <hi rendition="#italic">Oran</hi> and <hi
                                        rendition="#italic">Guazú</hi>, 31st January (? meaning
                                    which sailed 31st), 300 tons, which are (now) nearing Bordeaux
                                    with a view to change the flag, may be spared if possible, or
                                    else sunk without a trace being left (<hi rendition="#italic"
                                        >spurlos versenkt</hi>).</p>
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Luxburg</hi>
                                </p>
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#italic">July 3, 1917.</hi>
                                </p>
                                <p>No. 59. I learn from a reliable source that the Acting Minister
                                    for Foreign Affairs, who is a notorious ass and anglophil,
                                    declared in a secret session of the Senate that Argentina would
                                    demand from Berlin a promise not to sink more Argentine ships.
                                    If not agreed to, relations would be broken off. I recommend
                                    refusal and, if necessary, calling in the mediation of
                                    Spain.</p>
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Luxburg</hi>
                                </p>
                                <pb facs="0495" n="323" xml:id="pg323"/>
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#italic">July 9, 1917.</hi>
                                </p>
                                <p>No. 64. Without showing any tendency to make concessions,
                                    postpone reply to Argentine note until receipt of further
                                    reports. A change in Ministry is probable. As regards Argentine
                                    steamers, I recommend either compelling them to turn back,
                                    sinking them without leaving any traces, or letting them
                                    through. They are all quite small.</p>
                                <p rendition="#right">
                                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Luxbubg</hi>
                                </p>
                            </quote>
                        </p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
